# =============================================================================
# anoon — PRODUCTION environment template (for compose.prod.yml)
#
#   cp .env.prod.example .env     # on the server, in /opt/anoon/server-stack
#   chmod 600 .env                # then fill in real values
#
# EVERY value below is a placeholder. Generate FRESH prod secrets — never reuse
# dev values, never commit the filled-in .env (it is git-ignored).
# Keep an offline copy of the filled file in a password manager.
# `./rotate-secrets.sh` generates a complete set of fresh values for you.
#
# Each entry is tagged:
#   [REQUIRED]  the stack does not come up (or comes up unsafe) without it
#   [OPTIONAL]  empty is a supported mode; the comment says what empty means
#   [DEFAULTED] has a working default; override only for a reason
#
# Completeness: this file lists every variable actually read by the four
# services — companion (internal/config/config.go), Tinode (its config.template
# via server-stack/Dockerfile), the admin panel (admin/src/**), and the frontend
# build args — that matters in production. Tinode knobs left at their image
# defaults (FCM/TNPG push, AWS S3 media, cluster, pprof) are listed at the
# bottom under "not used yet" rather than silently omitted.
# =============================================================================

# ---- Public identity --------------------------------------------------------
# [REQUIRED] The one public domain the user app is served from (no scheme, no
# slash). DNS A (and AAAA if you have IPv6) records must point at the server
# BEFORE first start, or Let's Encrypt issuance fails.
DOMAIN=app.example.com
# [REQUIRED] Hostname of the admin panel. Separate host, not a path: separate
# cookie scope + its own basic-auth lock. Needs its own DNS A record.
ADMIN_DOMAIN=admin.example.com
# [REQUIRED] Contact email for the Let's Encrypt (ACME) account — cert notices.
ACME_EMAIL=admin@example.com
# [REQUIRED] Companion CORS allowlist (comma-separated exact origins, scheme
# included). Single-origin setup → just the app origin. The admin panel does
# NOT belong here: its browser talks only to its own Next.js route handlers,
# which call the companion server-side (no Origin header, no CORS). Companion
# refuses to start when this is empty or contains "*" while ENV=prod.
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://app.example.com

# ---- Postgres ---------------------------------------------------------------
# [REQUIRED] Superuser password for the in-stack postgres (never exposed
# off-network). Generate: openssl rand -hex 24
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME_openssl_rand_hex_24

# ---- Tinode security keys ---------------------------------------------------
# ALL FOUR must be fresh prod values (dev values are burned / upstream-known).
#
# [REQUIRED] API_KEY_SALT — MUST be rotated off the upstream default before
# launch; the default salt ships in the public Tinode source, so the api-key
# derived from it is effectively public. Generate: openssl rand -base64 32
API_KEY_SALT=CHANGE_ME_openssl_rand_base64_32
# [REQUIRED] Client api-key produced by Tinode's keygen with the salt above
# (build it from source: cd ../server && go build ./keygen). Salt and api-key
# only work as a pair. Baked into the frontend image at BUILD time — rebuild
# the frontend after changing it.
TINODE_API_KEY=CHANGE_ME_keygen_output
# [REQUIRED] HMAC key for Tinode auth tokens. Rotating later = all users
# logged out (recoverable). Generate: openssl rand -base64 32
AUTH_TOKEN_KEY=CHANGE_ME_openssl_rand_base64_32
# [REQUIRED] UID obfuscation key. Set ONCE and NEVER change: rotation breaks
# every existing user id. Generate: openssl rand -base64 16
UID_ENCRYPTION_KEY=CHANGE_ME_openssl_rand_base64_16

# ---- Companion --------------------------------------------------------------
# [REQUIRED] ROOT bot account: register it via POST /auth/register on first
# deploy, then promote with Tinode's init-db --make_root (DEPLOY-PROD.md step 6).
COMPANION_ROOT_LOGIN=anoonbot
# [REQUIRED] Generate: openssl rand -base64 24
COMPANION_ROOT_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_openssl_rand_base64_24
# [REQUIRED in prod] Shared secret between the admin panel and the companion
# /admin/* API. Empty disables the admin API entirely (503) — which, with the
# admin container deployed, means a panel that shows nothing. Both sides read
# this same variable. Generate: openssl rand -base64 24
COMPANION_ADMIN_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_openssl_rand_base64_24
# [REQUIRED in prod] HMAC key for per-operator admin tokens (X-Admin-Token).
# MUST be byte-identical to ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET below — it is the same HS256
# key the admin service signs operator sessions with, and the companion
# verifies forwarded tokens with.
#
# Empty selects LEGACY mode, where the operator id and role arrive as plain
# X-Admin-Id / X-Admin-Role headers: asserted by the caller, not attested.
# In legacy mode anyone holding COMPANION_ADMIN_SECRET can claim super_admin
# and moderation-log authorship is advisory. That is acceptable on a local
# stand and NOT acceptable in production — set it.
# Must be >=16 characters; companion refuses to start below that (a truncated
# key still selects attested mode and would 401 every admin request).
COMPANION_ADMIN_TOKEN_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_same_value_as_ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET
# [OPTIONAL] Shared secret Tinode presents on the companion /auth/rest hook
# (the callback its `rest` auth scheme makes for Google sign-in). REQUIRED once
# Google sign-in is enabled; empty keeps the hook disabled (503).
# Use HEX ONLY — the secret is embedded in the Tinode config's rest server_url
# (http://tinode:<SECRET>@companion:8080/auth/rest), so URL-reserved characters
# would corrupt it. Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
COMPANION_REST_SECRET=
# [OPTIONAL] Google OAuth client id (Web application, from Google Cloud Console).
# The browser flow needs the site listed under "Authorized JavaScript origins",
# NOT a redirect URI — the ID token is handed to a JS callback and no redirect
# ever happens. Empty disables Google sign-in on both sides. Not a secret: the
# same value is baked into the frontend bundle as NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID
# (compose.prod.yml reads this one variable for both).
COMPANION_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# [OPTIONAL] Every Google client id whose tokens companion accepts, comma-separated.
# Set this INSTEAD of the line above once the phone app ships. The web client id
# is not enough on its own: Google refuses a custom-scheme redirect for a client
# of type "Web", so Android and iOS each need their own OAuth client, and each
# stamps its own id into the token's "aud". A token minted by the app is rejected
# as "audience mismatch" unless its id is listed here.
# Order: web, Android, iOS. COMPANION_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID above stays the fallback
# when this is empty, so an existing deployment keeps working untouched.
# The Android and iOS ids also go into the app build as
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID_ANDROID / _IOS (mobile/eas.json).
COMPANION_GOOGLE_CLIENT_IDS=
# [DEFAULTED] Roulette anti-repeat window (Go duration). Prod default: 2h.
ROULETTE_RECENT_WINDOW=2h
# [DEFAULTED] Temp-ban expiry sweep interval; <=0 disables the background job.
BAN_SWEEP_INTERVAL=60s
# [REQUIRED in prod] Abuse guard: token-bucket limit on the public endpoints
# (auth, registration, roulette enqueue, reports, friend search), applied per
# user AND per client IP. Do NOT leave these empty — the CODE default is 0,
# which means NO rate limiting at all. 5 rps / burst 20 is far above normal UI
# traffic and far below a useful flood; raise only if real users hit 429s.
RATE_LIMIT_RPS=5
RATE_LIMIT_BURST=20

# ---- Companion: outbound email (currently stubbed) ---------------------------
# [OPTIONAL] Password-reset / verification mail. The transport is STUBBED today
# (internal/mail logs instead of sending), so these change nothing until it is
# wired. Listed for completeness — companion does read them.
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=
SMTP_FROM=no-reply@example.com

# ---- Admin panel -------------------------------------------------------------
# [REQUIRED] HS256 key the panel signs its httpOnly operator-session cookie
# with, >=16 chars (the panel refuses to start below that; use 32+).
# MUST equal COMPANION_ADMIN_TOKEN_SECRET above — that pairing is what makes
# the companion trust the operator id/role instead of taking the caller's word
# for them. Generate: openssl rand -base64 32
ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_same_value_as_COMPANION_ADMIN_TOKEN_SECRET
# [REQUIRED] Supabase project holding the operator accounts (email + argon2id
# password hash + optional TOTP) the panel logs in against. External service —
# create the project and paste its URL + secret (service-role) key. The key
# bypasses RLS: server-only, never in a NEXT_PUBLIC_* var.
SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=CHANGE_ME_supabase_service_role_key
# [OPTIONAL] Legacy broadcast relay (admin → WEB_URL/api/push/broadcast). Unused
# while ADMIN_BACKEND=companion, which compose pins. Leave empty.
ADMIN_BROADCAST_SECRET=
WEB_URL=
# [REQUIRED] Basic-auth gate Caddy puts in FRONT of the panel (second lock).
# Hash the password with:
#   docker run --rm caddy:2-alpine caddy hash-password --plaintext 'YOUR-PASSWORD'
# and paste the $2a$... digest below. The plaintext never goes in this file.
# NOTE: the hash contains $ characters. compose passes this through as an env
# value (no interpolation issue here because Caddy — not compose — expands it),
# but if you ever inline it into a compose file directly, double every $.
ADMIN_BASIC_AUTH_USER=admin
ADMIN_BASIC_AUTH_HASH=CHANGE_ME_caddy_hash_password_output
#
# Bootstrap of the FIRST operator account is NOT done through this file — it
# runs admin/scripts/create-admin.mjs once, with ADMIN_EMAIL / ADMIN_PASSWORD
# passed on that single command line (the script stores an argon2id hash, never
# the plaintext). See DEPLOY-PROD.md step 7. Do not persist ADMIN_PASSWORD here.

# ---- Web Push (VAPID) -------------------------------------------------------
# [OPTIONAL] Generate a FRESH prod P-256 keypair:
#   npx web-push generate-vapid-keys
# Public key is safe to expose; private key is a secret. Both empty = push
# disabled (safe no-op). Rotating these invalidates every existing browser
# subscription — users must re-subscribe.
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# [DEFAULTED] VAPID contact claim (mailto: or https:) push services may use.
VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:admin@example.com

# ---- TURN relay (coturn) — WebRTC calls behind NAT ----------------------------
# [REQUIRED for calls] Long-term-credential auth with ONE static user. These
# same values are baked into the frontend bundle (NEXT_PUBLIC_TURN_* below),
# i.e. they are PUBLIC by design — server-side quotas/denied-peer ranges bound
# the abuse (see coturn/turnserver.conf). Rotate occasionally: change here +
# rebuild the frontend image.
TURN_USER=anoon
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 16
TURN_PASS=CHANGE_ME_openssl_rand_hex_16
# TURN realm — use the app domain.
TURN_REALM=app.example.com

# Frontend copies (read by RTC_CONFIG at BUILD time — keep in sync with the
# three values above; changing any of them requires a frontend image rebuild).
# turn:<DOMAIN>:3478 covers UDP+TCP; clients fall back to turns:<DOMAIN>:5349
# (TLS) on networks that block 3478.
NEXT_PUBLIC_TURN_URL=turn:app.example.com:3478
NEXT_PUBLIC_TURN_USER=anoon
NEXT_PUBLIC_TURN_PASS=CHANGE_ME_same_as_TURN_PASS

# ---- DB lifecycle (leave false in normal operation) --------------------------
# [DEFAULTED] UPGRADE_DB=true only when bumping the Tinode version — BACK UP
# FIRST. RESET_DB=true WIPES the tinode database. Never on live prod.
RESET_DB=false
UPGRADE_DB=false

# ---- Build layout override (usually not needed) ------------------------------
# [OPTIONAL] Paths of the frontend / admin trees relative to server-stack/. The
# server layout (/opt/anoon/{server,server-stack,frontend,admin}) matches the
# defaults; on a dev workstation with frontend one level higher use:
# FRONTEND_DIR=../../frontend
#FRONTEND_DIR=../frontend
#ADMIN_DIR=../admin

# =============================================================================
# Pinned by compose.prod.yml — deliberately NOT settable here
# =============================================================================
# These are hard-coded in compose.prod.yml because a production deploy must not
# be able to get them wrong from a .env edit:
#   ENV=prod                 gates every other safety check in the companion
#   COMPANION_DEV_AUTH=0     the X-Anoon-Uid header bypass must never ship
#   SAMPLE_DATA=""           no demo users seeded into a live database
#   TLS_ENABLED=false        Caddy terminates HTTPS, not Tinode
#   MEDIA_HANDLER=fs         local uploads volume (switch to s3 per DEPLOY-PLAN §4)
#   FS_CORS_ORIGINS          derived from DOMAIN
#   SMTP_HOST_URL            derived from DOMAIN (links in verification mail)
#   ADMIN_BACKEND=companion  the panel reads live data, not Supabase fixtures
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_DATA_MODE=api  build arg; a "mock" admin image is refused
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SAME_ORIGIN=1 / NEXT_PUBLIC_USE_TINODE=1   frontend build args
#
# =============================================================================
# Read by Tinode but left at image defaults (not used yet)
# =============================================================================
# Listed so nobody has to grep the Dockerfile to find out whether they exist:
#   AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID / AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY / AWS_REGION / AWS_S3_BUCKET /
#   AWS_S3_ENDPOINT / AWS_CORS_ORIGINS   — only when MEDIA_HANDLER=s3
#   FCM_* / TNPG_*                       — Tinode's own push transports; anoon
#                                          uses its own Web Push in companion
#   SMTP_SERVER / SMTP_PORT / SMTP_SENDER / SMTP_LOGIN / SMTP_PASSWORD /
#   SMTP_AUTH_MECHANISM / SMTP_HELO_HOST / SMTP_DOMAINS /
#   EMAIL_VERIFICATION_REQUIRED / DEBUG_EMAIL_VERIFICATION_CODE
#                                        — Tinode-side mail (separate from the
#                                          companion SMTP_* block above)
#   WEBRTC_ENABLED / ICE_SERVERS_FILE    — Tinode's own call signalling; anoon
#                                          signals calls through the companion
#   CLUSTER_SELF / PPROF_URL / ACC_GC_ENABLED / SERVER_STATUS_PATH
#                                        — single-node deploy, all off
